{"name":"Salvia Divinorum","hex":"#929752","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"salvia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant in the genus Salvia, such as sage.\n\nSalvia divinorum, a plant used for its hallucinogenic properties; diviner's sage.","Salvia divinorum, a plant used for its hallucinogenic properties; diviner's sage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/salvia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salvia divinorum","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia divinorum is a species of plant in the sage genus Salvia, known for its transient psychoactive properties when its leaves, or extracts made from the leaves, are administered by smoking, chewing, or drinking. The leaves contain the potent compound salvinorin A and can induce a dissociative state and hallucinations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Salvia_divinorum_-_Herba_de_Maria.jpg/330px-Salvia_divinorum_-_Herba_de_Maria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia_divinorum"},{"title":"Salvia","blocks":[{"text":"Salvia is the largest genus of plants in the sage family Lamiaceae, with just under 1,000 species of shrubs, herbaceous perennials, and annuals. Within the Lamiaceae, Salvia is part of the tribe Mentheae within the subfamily Nepetoideae. One of several genera commonly referred to as sage, it includes two widely used herbs, Salvia officinalis and Salvia rosmarinus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Salvia_officinalis0.jpg/330px-Salvia_officinalis0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvia"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Huautla_de_Jimenez.jpg/330px-Huautla_de_Jimenez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Huautla_de_Jimenez.jpg/330px-Huautla_de_Jimenez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mazatec people performing a ritual dance in Huautla de Jimenez","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuautla_de_Jimenez.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Jbjohnson.jpg/500px-Jbjohnson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Jbjohnson.jpg/500px-Jbjohnson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jean Basset Johnson, who studied Mazatec shamanism during the 1930s","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJbjohnson.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg/500px-Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg/1280px-Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg/500px-Salvia_divinorum_-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flowering Salvia divinorum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalvia_divinorum_-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg/500px-Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg/1280px-Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg/500px-Salvia_divinorum_flower_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parse